  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Aloke Kumar Ghosh vs State Of West Bengal & Anr on 27 April, 2016 Author: Joymalya Bagchi Bench: Joymalya Bagchi In The High Court At Calcutta Criminal Appellate Jurisdiction Present: The Hon'ble Justice Joymalya Bagchi C.R.R. 1664 of 1998 Aloke Kumar Ghosh -Vs- State of West Bengal & Anr. For the Petitioner : Mr. Swapan Mullick, Mr. Jagatbandhu Roy, Ms. Manasi Roy For the State : Mr. Manjit Singh ... Ld. Public Prosecutor Mr. Pawan Gupta Heard on : 27.04.2016 Judgement on: 27.04.2016",
    "facts": "The prosecution case as alleged against the petitioner is to the effect that on 31st August, 1992 at 11 A.M., the complainant along with others had come to Premises No. 183B, Dalmadal Road, Rashtala, Bishnupur and found the petitioner was selling grocery items including chilli powder. Chilli powder contained in polythene packet, each weighing 50 gms., branded as 'Guin Spices', manufactured by Guin Traders, Aloke Kumar Ghosh vs State Of West Bengal & Anr on 27 April, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/154040285/ 1 Bishnupur, Bankura were found exposed for sale. On demand, the petitioner was unable to produce food licence and the complainant collected chilli powder samples of 450 gms. amounting to Rs.22.50p suspecting it to be adulterated. Notice was issued upon the petitioner. Sale receipts were also seized. It is the prosecution case that the petitioner was unable to produce any warranty for purchase of chilli powder at the time of inspection. It is alleged that report of public analyst showed that the chilli powder was adulterated and misbranded. On the basis of complaint lodged by PW1, trial commenced against the petitioner and one Madan Ghosh, owner of the shop. In conclusion of trial, petitioner was convicted and sentenced but co-accused Madan Ghosh was acquitted. Hence, the present revision.",
    "arguments": "Mr. Swapan Mullick, learned advocate appearing for the petitioner submitted that the courts below did not consider the cash memo which was exhibited in the instant case and which showed that the articles had been purchased from DW1. He further submitted that the report of public analyst (Exhibit-13) does not establish that the seized article is adulterated.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Mr. Swapan Mullick, learned advocate appearing for the petitioner submitted that the courts below did not consider the cash memo which was exhibited in the instant case and which showed that the articles had been purchased from DW1. He further submitted that the report of public analyst (Exhibit-13) does not establish that the seized article is adulterated.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
